ix}"] [:db/cas 17592186264833 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186264841 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 6 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 9 & -2 & 6\\end{aligned}]" "\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}9 & 0 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 6 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 9 & -2 & 6\\end{array}\\right]"] [:db/cas 17592186265210 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 18 & 27 & -9\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 18 & 27 & -9\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186265796 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}$ on $A?$" "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}$ on $A?$"] [:db/cas 17592186265796 :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}$ means that we need to multiply the third row of the matrix by $\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nMultiplying the $3$rd row by $\\frac{1}{3}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 6 & \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9 & \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The operation ${R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}$ means that we need to multiply the third row of the matrix by $\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nMultiplying the $3$rd row by $\\frac{1}{3}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 9 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 6 & \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9 & \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265809 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $C,$ given by\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the following row operations on $C?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Consider the matrix $C,$ given by\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the following row operations on $C?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265809 :question/worked-solution "Let's list the required operations:\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}$ means that we need to add $-2$ times the second row to the first row.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}$ means that we need to add $1$ times the second row to the third row.\n\nApplying the operations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0+(-2)(5) & 4+(-2)(2) & 7+(-2)(1) \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(1)\\cdot {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1+(1)(5) & 3+(1)(2) & 0+(1)(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's list the required operations:\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}$ means that we need to add $-2$ times the second row to the first row.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}$ means that we need to add $1$ times the second row to the third row.\n\nApplying the operations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0+(-2)(5) & 4+(-2)(2) & 7+(-2)(1) \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(1)\\cdot {R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1+(1)(5) & 3+(1)(2) & 0+(1)(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265810 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $C,$ given by\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the following row operations on $C?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Consider the matrix $C,$ given by\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the following row operations on $C?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265810 :question/worked-solution "Let's list the required operations:\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{3}$ means that we need to add $3$ times the third row to the first row.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{3}$ means that we need to add $5$ times the third row to the second row.\n\nApplying the operations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1+(3)(2) & 2+(3)(0) & 1+(3)(-1) \\\\ -9 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -9 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -9+(5)(2) & 0+(5)(0) & -4+(5)(-1) \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's list the required operations:\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{3}$ means that we need to add $3$ times the third row to the first row.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{3}$ means that we need to add $5$ times the third row to the second row.\n\nApplying the operations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1+(3)(2) & 2+(3)(0) & 1+(3)(-1) \\\\ -9 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -9 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -9+(5)(2) & 0+(5)(0) & -4+(5)(-1) \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265811 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 5 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -9 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ on $A?$" "Consider the augmented matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}4 & -1 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 5 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -9 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ on $A?$"] [:db/cas 17592186265811 :question/worked-solution "The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ means that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 5 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -9 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -9 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ means that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}4 & -1 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 5 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -9 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}6 & 5 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -9 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265812 :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $V,$ given by\n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 9 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 6 & 7 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 5 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the following row operations on $V?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Consider the augmented matrix $V,$ given by\n\n$$\nV=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 9 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 6 & 7 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 5 & 7\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the following row operations on $V?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265812 :question/worked-solution "Let's list the required operations:\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}$ means that we need to swap the first and third rows.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3}$ means that we need to add $-1$ times the third row to the second row.\n\nApplying the operations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 9 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 6 & 7 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 5 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 6 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 9 & -2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 4+(-1)(-1) & 4+(-1)(9) & 6+(-1)(-2) & 7+(-1)(6) \\\\ -1 & 9 & -2 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 8 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 9 & -2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's list the required operations:\n\n- The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}$ means that we need to swap the first and third rows.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3}$ means that we need to add $-1$ times the third row to the second row.\n\nApplying the operations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 9 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 6 & 7 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 5 & 7\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}9 & 0 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 6 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 9 & -2 & 6\\end{array}\\right]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}9 & 0 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 4+(-1)(-1) & 4+(-1)(9) & 6+(-1)(-2) & 7+(-1)(6) \\\\ -1 & 9 & -2 & 6\\end{array}\\right] \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}9 & 0 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 8 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 9 & -2 & 6\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265820 :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $A$ into the matrix $B$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 10 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $A$ into the matrix $B$, where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 10 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265820 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st and the $2$nd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $3$rd row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that modify the $3$rd row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 10 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {R}_{3}.\n$$" "First, notice that the $1$st and the $2$nd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $3$rd row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that modify the $3$rd row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 10 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {R}_{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265822 :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix  $C$ into the matrix $D$, where \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix  $C$ into the matrix $D$, where \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265822 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $1$st row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that modify the $1$st row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=3\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=3\\cdot {R}_{1}.\n$$" "First, notice that the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has changed is the $1$st row. \n\nBy considering the given row operations that modify the $1$st row, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=3\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=3\\cdot {R}_{1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265824 :question/problem "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $E$ into the matrix $F$, where\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,F=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" "Which of the following elementary row operations transforms the matrix $E$ into the matrix $F$, where\n\n$$\nE=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,F=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265824 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st row is the same in both matrices. The only rows that have changed are the $2$nd row and the $3$rd row.   \n\nBy considering the given row operations that change both the $2$nd and the $3$rd row, we notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =F.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}.\n$$" "First, notice that the $1$st row is the same in both matrices. The only rows that have changed are the $2$nd row and the $3$rd row.   \n\nBy considering the given row operations that change both the $2$nd and the $3$rd row, we notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =\\begin{bmatr